From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Norman Herms <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 16:03:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: allwinner: a733: Give IOMMU0 the CSI and ISP port
 gates

A master's register block in the IOMMU does not answer while the
master's port on the MBUS matrix is gated, and the access stalls the
bus. The IOMMU driver takes a reference on the port gate of a master,
given as a "masterN" clock, for as long as the master translates. The
A733 clock driver marks these gates CLK_IS_CRITICAL, so they stay
enabled and the reference does not change their state.

Name the port gate of the camera input, master 1 (CSI) and master 2
(ISP) of the first IOMMU (manual chapter 10). Both are in the video
input system and use the same MBUS port, VID_IN, bit 24 of
MBUS_MAT_CLK_GATING_REG (manual section 4.1.6.83).

Nothing changes until a node lists the IOMMU in "iommus": the driver
enables a master clock only for a master with a user.

Not covered: the manual does not say which matrix port the two masters
use; VID_IN is the only port of the video input system.

Tested on Radxa Cubie A7S: with both nodes enabled for a test, IOMMU0
holds the VID_IN port gate for masters 1 and 2, and both devices
translate through it.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733.dtsi | 4 +++-
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733.dtsi
index 36f730e32d63..75c150ed9a85 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733.dtsi
@@ -1110,9 +1110,11 @@ iommu0: iommu@3900000 {
 				 <&ccu CLK_APB_IOMMU0_SYS>,
 				 <&ccu CLK_AHB_IOMMU0_SYS>,
 				 <&ccu CLK_MBUS_IOMMU0>,
+				 <&ccu CLK_MBUS_VID_IN>,
+				 <&ccu CLK_MBUS_VID_IN>,
 				 <&ccu CLK_MBUS_VE_ENC0_GATE>;
 			clock-names = "mclk", "pclk", "hclk", "mbus",
-				      "master3";
+				      "master1", "master2", "master3";
 			resets = <&ccu RST_BUS_IOMMU0_SYS>;
 			#iommu-cells = <1>;
 		};
